<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramón Rosa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramón Rosa (1848–1893) was a Honduran lawyer, writer and liberal statesman who served as chief minister to his cousin, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-aurelio-soto">Marco Aurelio Soto</a>, from 1876 to 1883. Together they carried out the Liberal Reform, reorganising the treasury, schools, postal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> services and legal codes and inviting foreign investment in mining. He was also a leading essayist of nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramon-rosa.jpg" alt="Ramón Rosa" width="251" height="283" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JVC3ETA · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramon_Rosa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramón Rosa Soto, son of Juan José Soto and Isidora Rosa, took a degree in philosophy at the National University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a> and then studied law at the National University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, where his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-aurelio-soto">Marco Aurelio Soto</a> was a classmate and the historian José Milla y Vidaurre one of his teachers. He graduated in October 1871. Shortly before, he had joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> and attacked the Conservative government of Marshal Vicente Cerna in El Central, a newspaper he co-founded, which backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-garcia-granados">Miguel García Granados</a> after the Liberal Reform triumphed in Guatemala in June 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rosa served as deputy director of finance under García Granados and then held the education ministry under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a>, alternating it and foreign affairs with Soto. Strongly anticlerical, the two carried through the separation of church and state, civil marriage and civil registration, the secularisation of cemeteries, free and secular primary schooling and a university without theology. In 1876 Barrios helped install Soto in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, and the cousins landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amapala">Amapala</a> to form a provisional government.</p><p>As Soto's minister general, Rosa applied the Guatemalan model to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, reorganising its administration and laws and opening it to foreign capital, above all from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; in December 1879 Soto founded the Rosario Mining Company with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> businessman S. Valentine. Rosa gave the law and the schools the stamp of positivist philosophy, expressed in the Code of Public Instruction of 1882, and after hopes for a national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> economy failed he sought foreign investment in mining and agriculture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Barrios's troops on the border forced Soto to resign in May 1883, Rosa went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>, where he lived in Alajuela in 1885 and 1886, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> in 1889, founded the journal Guacerique and proposed a Progressive Party for the elections of 1891, which soon dissolved. He died on 28 May 1893. His essays include Social Constitution in Honduras and The Political Parties, and he wrote biographies of José Milla y Vidaurre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-cecilio-del-valle">José Cecilio del Valle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a>. His portrait appears on the 500 lempira note.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramón Rosa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marco Aurelio Soto, president from 1876 to 1883, and his minister <b>Ramón Rosa</b> laid the foundations of the modern state: they reorganised the treasury, schools and legal codes, made Tegucigalpa the capital in 1880 and adopted a liberal constitution that year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.11,-87.21&amp;year=1877" aria-label="Tegucigalpa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ramón Rosa"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5232 3806h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5232 3806h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.11,-87.21&amp;year=1877">What was here in 1877? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
